Gerhard,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on reserves, farms and remote sites; we don't sell packages.

Your teams protect reserves where the network is usually the weak link. Thermal cameras, drones, fence and trigger alarms and a control-room feed are only as good as the backhaul carrying them, and on most Eastern Cape reserves that backhaul stops at the lodge. What we build is the part in between: ridge high sites, a monitored backbone across the property and one carrier uplink, owned by the reserve.

We've just run desktop terrain studies on a set of reserves between Makhanda, Kenton-on-Sea and Paterson (Pumba, Buffalo Kloof, Fort Governor's, Amakhala, Sibuya among them), mapping the ridge sites and the clear radio paths to each lodge and to the field. Sandymount Safaris runs on a network we built: over 800 Mbps across their own backbone and the provider callouts went to zero.

The reason I'm writing is that a reserve that sees an anti-poaching unit and a network proposal together sees one security solution rather than two suppliers. Where your control room loses sight of the field on a reserve you cover, we can usually show on paper within a day whether that can be fixed, and it costs the reserve nothing to find out.

Could we meet for coffee in Gqeberha this week? I'll bring the studies.

